module std.error_primitives

import std.float { Float }

type Result<ok, err> = Ok { value: ok } | Err { value: err }

type DivError = DivideByZero | Overflow

// THE UNINHABITED TYPE, AND WHY IT LIVES HERE RATHER THAN BESIDE Unit. std.types imports
// std.algebra, so algebra cannot import types. This module imports nothing, so both can name
// Never. A dag-tree module may import it (v2.std.cross_tree.import_model: dag may not import v2).
// An arm instantiated at Never has no constructor: the binding declares that arm unreachable for
// that subject (std.goal_assessment AssessRefusal). Never has no CONSTRUCTOR. Its one inhabitant is
// divergence: pure_dag_seam_unreachable below never returns, so typing it Never is honest.
type Never

// THE ELIMINATOR. Zero arms are exhaustive iff the scrutinee is an uninhabited sum (a coproduct
// with zero arms, or this Never declaration by identity). An opaque without dag constructors is
// not uninhabited. absurd inhabits every T; it cannot be applied to an Int. It consumes a Never;
// it does not mint one.
fn absurd<T>(n: Never) -> T {
  match (n) {
  }
}

// BOTTOM IS NOT A BYTES FACT, SO IT LIVES IN THE LEAF. The divergent seam and its result-type
// projections lived in std.bytes, which imports std.types, which imports std.algebra -- so
// std.algebra could not name the seam and spelled its own divergence as an unnamed `1 / 0`. This
// module imports nothing, so every std module (std.algebra included) can import the seam without a
// cycle.

// THE DIVERGENT SEAM IS A DECLARED HOST SEAM, NOT A DIVISION. Its job is to be bottom: a value an
// arm the author proved unreachable can hold, which diverges if the proof was wrong. It was spelled
// `1 / 0`, and that spelling carried a meaning to each realization that it did not declare: the
// interpreter refused with DivisionByZero only if reached, while rustc's deny-by-default
// unconditional_panic lint refused every emitted crate whose closure contained the module, reached
// or not (native App Attest, gunbc#12251). Relaxing that lint for generated code would have removed
// a real check to admit one placeholder (review 71098 of gunbc#12261). As a rostered
// HostRealizedSeam each realization states the divergence itself: the interpreter's
// free_call.pure_dag_seam_unreachable arm refuses with a typed error naming the seam, and
// v1_rt::pure_dag_seam_unreachable panics with the same name -- an intentional divergence, which is
// what `!` is for, rather than a constant expression the target's own checker must reject.
// THE SEAM IS TYPED NEVER: it never returns, and divergence is the honest inhabitant of the empty type.
// This is not the never() #14022 deleted: that was a CONSTRUCTOR of Never, a value of the empty type,
// which is unsound. A call that diverges produces no value at all. One rostered seam (the
// std.primitive_projection HostRealizedSeam row, the interpreter's free_call arm, v1_rt's `-> !`)
// carries the divergence for every result type, through absurd.
fn pure_dag_seam_unreachable() -> Never {
  pure_dag_seam_unreachable()
}

// ONE SEAM, ONE ELIMINATION PER RESULT TYPE. A divergent expression has no result type to project from,
// and .dag has no implicit bottom-to-T at a call site, so absurd IS that coercion and is written once per
// result type, here. History these projections must not regress: typing the seam Int alone was a silent
// lie in the simd and wgsl float kernels, which declared List<Float> and produced List<Int> (codex review
// 45398); `1.0 / 0.0` is IEEE-754 +inf, not a trap, so a Float projection written that way RETURNED a
// fabricated plausible value (codex review 45493); and "" is a perfectly good String, which is what made a
// literal-returning String projection a fabrication. The next form forced the Int seam in an `if`
// condition and returned a dead literal from both arms, 0.0 and "", present only to give the expression
// its type; a float literal has no ruled home on the native route (infer_reason_float_literal_home_unruled,
// a separate open item not decided here). Now every projection is absurd over the one Never seam, which
// diverges before any value of the result type exists, so no literal stands in for a value.
//
// WHY THE PROJECTIONS ARE NOT DELETED INTO THEIR CALLERS. rustc under -D warnings refuses any call whose
// argument has an uninhabited type (unreachable call / unreachable expression) at the CALLER, so
// absurd(pure_dag_seam_unreachable()) may appear only in an item that carries allow(unreachable_code)
// itself: these three, named in std.primitive_projection never_projection_declarations (the precedent of
// #14022's item-level allow on absurd), and no caller crate gains an allow.
fn pure_dag_seam_unreachable_int() -> Int {
  absurd(pure_dag_seam_unreachable())
}

fn pure_dag_seam_unreachable_float() -> Float {
  absurd(pure_dag_seam_unreachable())
}

fn pure_dag_seam_unreachable_string() -> String {
  absurd(pure_dag_seam_unreachable())
}
